  ],
  "text": "Four and a half thousand years ago, Indus cities had grid streets and covered sewers. Plenty of cities today still do not have covered drainage. Mohenjo-daro was laid out on a grid, deliberately, before a single house went up. Houses had private bathrooms, and those drained into channels running under the street. The bricks are standardised. The same proportions, across hundreds of kilometres. Which means a standard existed, and somebody enforced it over that distance. And here is the strange absence. No palaces. No grand tombs. Nothing that looks like a king. Enormous public baths and granaries instead, and not a throne anywhere. Their writing is still undeciphered, so there is nobody left to ask. We know their plumbing intimately, and their language not at all. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
